# {{ project_name }}

{{ description }}

From signals or target weights to net returns: lagged trades, drift, rebalancing, costs, delistings,
and walk-forward evaluation. It runs as generated, and it is yours to change.

```bash
uv sync
uv run {{ project_name }} backtest            # momentum on random-walk demo prices
uv run {{ project_name }} walk-forward        # out of sample only
uv run {{ project_name }} report              # the latest run as Markdown, with a chart
```

## What to trade

| You have | Use |
| --- | --- |
| A signal in code | `signal` in `config.toml`, and `{{ package_name }}/firm/signals.py` for your own |
| A file of scores, such as alpha-research's | `--signal-file scores.csv` |
| Target weights, such as your optimizer's | `--weights-file weights.csv`, which skips the portfolio rule |

Each file has a date column, then one column per asset. Set `data` to your prices in the same shape.
Scores go through a portfolio rule (`exposure` in `config.toml`), which can be your own in
`{{ package_name }}/firm/rules.py`.

## The next stage

Each backtest folder holds `returns.csv` (net and gross) and `weights.csv`, with their provenance
beside them. A performance-report application reads `returns.csv` directly:

```bash
uv run my-report report --returns ../{{ project_name }}/results/RUN/returns.csv
```

## Make it yours

| Level | What you do | Where |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Set data, costs, and the schedule | `config.toml` |
| 2 | Add your own signals and portfolio rules | `{{ package_name }}/firm/` |
| 3 | Add your own file formats | `{{ package_name }}/firm/loaders.py` |
| 4 | Change anything else; you own the code | Everywhere, with the tests to catch mistakes |

`AGENTS.md` tells a coding assistant the same rules. See `LIMITATIONS.md` for what this does not do.
